Subglottic Stenosis
Respiratory System
Inspiratory stridor, History of intubation, Barking cough, Fixed upper airway obstruction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-046
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of noisy, difficult breathing and stridor on mild exertion in a patient with a history of prolonged ICU admission.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with an audible wheeze from the neck, progressive dyspnea, and a history of previous prolonged intubation. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate the severity of the upper airway obstruction and rule out impending airway collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My breathing sounds like a high-pitched whistle from my throat every time I take a step, and it feels like I am breathing through a tiny straw.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports progressive exertional dyspnea developing over the last six months, following a prolonged intubation and ICU stay for an unrelated trauma. Over the last 48 hours, they have experienced biphasic or inspiratory stridor that worsens significantly with exertion or agitation. They describe a brassy or barking cough and a feeling of a mechanical obstruction strictly localized in the neck. The patient notes hoarseness and a distinctly weak voice. They report recurrent episodes over the past month that were misdiagnosed at urgent care centers as refractory asthma.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently limiting all physical activity due to respiratory distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding suffocation; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, speaking in a weak, breathy voice. High-pitched inspiratory stridor is audible without a stethoscope when the patient moves.
Physical Exam: Auscultation of the neck reveals harsh, localized inspiratory and expiratory stridor over the larynx/trachea. Lower respiratory tract auscultation is completely normal, with clear, equal air entry and no peripheral wheeze or crackles. Mild suprasternal retractions are visible during respiratory distress. The clinical presentation strongly correlates with a fixed upper airway obstruction pattern secondary to structural stenosis.
Environmental Observations: Patient is sitting completely still to avoid triggering the stridor.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol inhaler prescribed previously for "asthma" utilized with absolute lack of efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subglottic Stenosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Adult-Onset Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Stridor is localized entirely to the neck rather than widespread expiratory wheeze in the chest, and symptoms are completely refractory to bronchodilators.

Vocal Cord Paralysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a strong barking cough (requires vocal cord adduction to generate pressure) despite the voice weakness.

Acute Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, progressive 6-month timeline, absence of high fever, and ability to swallow saliva normally without drooling.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark signs of subglottic stenosis: inspiratory stridor, a barking cough, and progressive dyspnea, all localized to the upper airway on a background of previous prolonged intubation. The normal chest auscultation combined with the harsh neck sounds confirms a fixed upper airway obstruction. The recent escalation of stridor with minimal exertion indicates critical narrowing of the tracheal lumen. Deferring assessment until the next in-hours period would have posed a significant risk of acute airway decompensation from minor mucosal edema. An urgent after-hours home visit was required to confirm the patency of the airway, cease ineffective asthma medications, and coordinate a rapid-access specialist referral for surgical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised absolute vocal rest and minimization of all physical exertion. Provided a referral for urgent ENT/Respiratory physician review for flow-volume loop spirometry and bronchoscopy.
Positive Behavioral Change: Breathe slowly and steadily in through your nose and out through pursed lips; actively slowing your respiratory rate prevents the air from becoming turbulent as it passes the narrowed section of your windpipe, directly reducing the noisy stridor and the sensation of choking.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent specialist review within 48 to 72 hours; patient must contact the ENT clinic in the morning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if breathing becomes distressed while sitting perfectly still, if your lips turn blue, or if you begin to feel faint or confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This clinical assessment strongly points to structural scar tissue in the windpipe; a camera test (bronchoscopy) is required by a specialist to visually confirm the narrowing.
Clinical Course: Inhalers will not fix this physical blockage; definitive treatment usually requires a minor surgical procedure to dilate or remove the scar tissue.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call for emergency help immediately if you feel you cannot pull enough air into your lungs to stay conscious.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The breathing tube used during your hospital stay months ago caused a small ring of scar tissue to form in your windpipe; this scar has slowly shrunk over time, creating a physical bottleneck that air must squeeze through.
Actionable Steps: Avoid any heavy lifting, running, or emotional stress; pushing large volumes of air rapidly through this bottleneck creates friction that can cause the tissues to swell and close the airway entirely.

